body:
### Tested versions

Godot v4.7.stable

### System information

Godot v4.7.stable.mono - Windows 11 (build 26200) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - OpenGL 3 (Compatibility) - NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Ti (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.9186) - AMD Ryzen 9 9900X 12-Core Processor (24 threads) - 31.12 GiB memory - WASAPI (48000 Hz, Stereo/mono)

### Issue description

Using `discard` inside the `light()` function of a spatial shader still produces different visual results depending on the renderer.

This looks related to #85370, which was closed by #87495. As far as I understand, that fix was intended to make `ALPHA` / `discard` in `light()` behave consistently across all three renderers. However, I can still reproduce renderer-dependent output with a minimal shader using `discard` in `light()`.

In my case:

```glsl
void light() {
	if (ATTENUATION < 0.0001) {
		discard;
	}

	DIFFUSE_LIGHT = vec3(1.0);
}
```

But a minimal shader (A light must be present in the scene for light() to be called) can just be:

```glsl
shader_type spatial;

void fragment() {
	ALBEDO = vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0);
}

void light() {
	discard;
}
```

Observed result:

Mobile: appears to produce the expected result (mesh completely disappeared with minimal shader).
Forward+: produces different output, with dark/black pixels where the discarded light contribution seems to behave differently (mesh is black with minimal shader).
Compatibility / OpenGL: produces a different result again, inconsistent with both Mobile and Forward+(mesh is white with minimal shader).

Comparison image:
<img width="1928" height="1189" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7ad65d20-4cbd-44a3-a7e8-46ca6dd86d73" />

Expected behavior:

discard inside light() should give result like moblie renderer and behave consistently across renderers.

Actual behavior:

The same shader and scene produce visibly different output in Mobile, Forward+, and Compatibility / OpenGL.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Create a new 3D project.
2. Add a `MeshInstance3D` with any visible mesh.
3. Assign a `ShaderMaterial` using this spatial shader:

```glsl
shader_type spatial;

void fragment() {
	ALBEDO = vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0);
}

void light() {
	discard;
}
```

4. Add a light to the scene, for example DirectionalLight3D, so that the light() function is called.
5. Run the scene using the Mobile renderer.
6. Run the same scene using Forward+.
7. Run the same scene using Compatibility / OpenGL.

Compare the rendered output.

### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)

[mrp.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/29780684/mrp.zip)

mrp is using Forward+ renderer, switch renderers in project setting to reproduce the issue.
